All plugins, including local plugins, observe events the same way. This is the Moodle page that talks about it: https://docs.moodle.org/dev/Event_2 To observe, you need to
do three things: * Tell Moodle which events you want to observe * Tell Moodle what function to run * Create the function In your plugin create the file `db/observer.php`. In this create an array, something like: 
```text
$observers = array(
    array(
        'eventname'   => '\core\event\user_created',
        'callback'    => '\local_myplugin\observers::user_created',
    )
);
```
 Then, create the file: `classes/observers.php` and this file will look something like: 
```text
namespace local_myplugin;
defined('MOODLE_INTERNAL') || die();
/**
 * Handles events
 */
 class observers {
     /**
      * Handles user created event
      * @param \core\event\user_created $event Event data
      */
      public static function user_created(\core\event\user_created $event) {
          // Do your thing.
      }
}
```
 You'll need to bump your version number for the new observer to be recognised by Moodle, but that's about it.

